    "q-350227": {
      "dom_id": "step-q350227",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Given that  $f(t)=2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t)\\,i-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\frac{6}{t}\\,k,$   what is ${f}^{′}(1)?$\n\n${f}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$i\\,\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$j\\,\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$k$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ by differentiating each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t))\\,i-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{6}{t})\\,k \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t)+2)\\,i-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-\\frac{6}{{t}^{2}}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, to compute ${f}^{′}(1),$ we substitute $t=1$ into the expression for ${f}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot 1)+2)\\,i-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)\\,j-\\frac{6}{{1}^{2}}\\,k \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\,i-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡1\\,j-6\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
